l point with digits on only one side (`.n` or `n.`) | [ ] |\n| C06 | `parse_number` | no options; lowercase exponent marker with signed or unsigned exponent | [ ] |\n| C07 | `parse_number` | no options; uppercase exponent marker with signed or unsigned exponent | [ ] |\n| C08 | `parse_number` | no options; immediate non-number delimiter after a valid prefix, exercising scanner `default` | [ ] |\n| C09 | `parse_number` | no options; `length` truncates a backing byte array without a NUL terminator | [ ] |\n| C10 | `parse_number` | no options; nonzero `offset` selects a valid numeric token inside prefix/suffix bytes | [ ] |\n| C11 | `parse_number` | no options; scanner accepts a longer malformed sequence but `strtod` consumes only a valid prefix (for example `1e+` or `1-2`) | [ ] |\n| C12 | `parse_number` | no options; zero and multi-byte leading-zero integer forms | [ ] |\n| C13 | `parse_number` | no options; finite value `>= INT_MAX`, including exact and one-past boundary, saturating `valueint` to `INT_MAX` | [ ] |\n| C14 | `parse_number` | no options; finite value `<= INT_MIN`, including exact and one-past boundary, saturating `valueint` to `INT_MIN` | [ ] |\n| C15 | `parse_number` | no options; extreme positive or negative exponent converted to infinity and saturated | [ ] |\n| C16 | `parse_number` | no options; extreme negative exponent converted to subnormal or underflowed zero | [ ] |\n| C17 | `parse_number` | no options; finite interior values adjacent to both `int` saturation boundaries | [ ] |\n| C18 | `parse_number` | no options; long numeric token (64 through 512 scanned bytes), token ends at `length` | [ ] |\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
